const P2_MODELS = {
 helper:{band:8, card:"helper", t:"Band 8 · Describe a person who often helps others",
  text:"The person I'd like to talk about is my neighbour, Tatiana Ivanovna, who's probably [[the most selfless person]] I've ever met. [[To give you a bit of background]], she's a retired chemistry teacher in her late sixties, and she's lived in the flat opposite ours [[for as long as I can remember]].\n\n[[What makes her stand out]] is that she [[goes out of her way]] to help everyone in our building, often without being asked. If someone's ill, she'll turn up with homemade soup; if a parent is running late, she'll [[keep an eye on]] their kids. [[On top of that]], she tutors teenagers in chemistry for free — when I was struggling with my school-leaving exams, she spent [[countless]] evenings explaining things to me at her kitchen table, and I [[honestly don't think I would have passed]] without her.\n\nAs for why she does it, I think it [[comes down to]] her personality. She once told me that helping people [[gives her a sense of purpose]] now that she's retired, and that she'd rather be useful than sit in front of the TV. [[I suppose]] it's also [[the way she was brought up]] — her parents were doctors in a small village, so [[looking after others]] was simply part of family life.\n\n[[Looking back]], I realise how lucky our building is to have her. She's [[a real role model]] for me, and [[if I'm half as generous]] as she is when I'm her age, I'll be pretty proud of myself. So all in all, she's someone I [[genuinely admire]].",
  why:["Opener с придаточным who + оценка","Конкретные детали: суп, дети, химия по вечерам","3-й тип условного: I don't think I would have passed without her","Cleft: What makes her stand out is…","Последний пункт («why») — самый длинный и развёрнутый","Связки: To give you a bit of background, On top of that, As for, Looking back, So all in all"]},
 helper6:{band:6, card:"helper", t:"Band 6 · та же карточка",
  text:"I want to talk about my neighbour. Her name is Tatiana and she is a retired teacher. She is about sixty-five years old and she lives near my flat.\n\nShe helps people very often. When my neighbours are ill, she brings them food. She also helps children with their homework. When I had exams at school, she helped me with chemistry. It was very useful for me and I passed my exam.\n\nI think she helps people because she is very kind. Also, she doesn't work now, so she has a lot of free time. Her parents were doctors, so maybe this is the reason.\n\nI like her very much because she is a good person. Everybody in our building likes her. I want to be like her in the future.",
  why:["Всего ~140 слов — это около минуты, а нужно почти две","Почти все предложения простые: подлежащее + глагол","Слова базовые: very kind, good person, very useful, a lot of","Связки простые: and, also, so, because","Нет придаточных, условных, причастных оборотов","Что сделать: добавить детали, чувства, 2–3 сложные структуры и слова band 7+"]},
 cityvisit:{band:8, card:"cityvisit", t:"Band 8 · Describe a city you enjoyed visiting",
  text:"There are quite a few cities I could talk about, but [[the one that really stands out]] is Kazan, which I visited with two friends last May.\n\n[[To give you a bit of background]], Kazan is about 800 kilometres east of Moscow, on the Volga, and it's often called Russia's third capital. We went there for a long weekend, mainly because one of my friends had grown up there and [[kept insisting]] we come and see it.\n\n[[What struck me straight away]] was how [[effortlessly]] the city blends two cultures. Inside the Kremlin there's a [[stunning]] blue-and-white mosque [[standing right next to]] an Orthodox cathedral, which I found [[genuinely moving]]. We spent most of the first day just [[wandering around]] the old town, and in the evening we [[took a boat trip]] along the river at sunset — the views were [[absolutely breathtaking]]. [[On top of that]], the food was incredible: my friend's mum [[treated us to]] homemade echpochmak, little triangular meat pies, and I [[could have eaten a dozen]].\n\nAs for why I enjoyed it so much, I think it's because it was [[a far cry from]] what I'd expected. I'd imagined a typical industrial city, but it turned out to be [[vibrant]], clean and [[surprisingly laid-back]]. [[If my friend hadn't dragged us there]], I probably [[would never have considered]] going.\n\nSo all in all, it was one of the best trips I've had, and I'd [[go back in a heartbeat]] — ideally in winter next time, to see it covered in snow.",
  why:["Opener band 8: There are quite a few … but the one that really stands out is…","Past perfect: had grown up, I'd imagined","Сенсорные детали: закат, пирожки, мечеть рядом с собором","3-й тип условного в оценке","Идиомы к месту: a far cry from, go back in a heartbeat"]},
 cantlive:{band:8, card:"cantlive", t:"Band 8 · Describe something you cannot live without",
  text:"I'm going to tell you about something I genuinely can't imagine my life without, which is my e-reader. It might sound a bit boring, but [[bear with me]].\n\nI've had it for about four years now. It was a birthday present from my parents, who'd [[got tired of]] me complaining about carrying three paperbacks in my rucksack every time I went on holiday. It's about the size of a small notebook, [[incredibly light]], and the battery [[lasts for weeks]], which is [[a lifesaver]] when I'm travelling.\n\nI use it pretty much every day. I read on the metro on the way to work — it's a forty-minute [[commute]], so that's [[well over an hour of reading a day]] — and I always read for twenty minutes or so before bed. [[What I love most about it]] is that I can look up any English word just by tapping on it, so it's [[been instrumental in]] building my vocabulary. In the last year alone, I've read around thirty books in English, which I [[would never have managed]] with paper books and a dictionary.\n\nAs for why I can't live without it, [[I suppose]] it's become part of my daily routine and [[a little escape]] from the noise of the city. Once I left it at a friend's house, and I felt completely lost for a week — I [[ended up]] staring at my phone instead, which [[left me feeling]] far more stressed.\n\nSo all in all, it's not the most exciting object in the world, but it's [[easily the most useful thing I own]].",
  why:["Present perfect для срока: I've had it for four years","Цифры и детали делают рассказ живым","Cleft: What I love most about it is…","Сравнение в конце: far more stressed, the most useful thing I own","Одна вещь подходит к 6–7 карточкам: подарок, покупка, гаджет, «не могу жить без»"]},
 decision:{band:8, card:"decision", t:"Band 8 · Describe an important decision that you made",
  text:"I'd like to tell you about a decision which, looking back, was [[a real turning point]] for me: leaving my law degree to study linguistics.\n\nThis happened about three years ago, when I was in my second year at university. [[On paper]], everything was going fine — my grades were decent and my parents were [[over the moon]] that I was going to be a lawyer. [[The trouble was]] that I [[dreaded]] every single lecture. The only classes I actually [[looked forward to]] were English and Latin, and I'd spend my evenings reading about languages instead of studying cases.\n\nIt wasn't an easy decision to make. I spent weeks [[weighing up the pros and cons]]: I'd lose a year, my parents would be disappointed, and I wasn't even sure I'd get a place on the linguistics course. [[In the end]], I talked it over with my favourite English teacher, who said something that [[stuck with me]]: if I stayed, I'd be building a whole career on something I didn't care about. That [[tipped the balance]], so I [[took the plunge]], applied, and to my surprise I got in.\n\nAs for why it was so important, it [[completely changed the direction]] of my life. I'm now studying something I'm [[passionate about]], I've started teaching English part-time, and I'm far more motivated than I ever was before. [[If I hadn't made that choice]], I'd probably [[be sitting]] in an office now, feeling miserable.\n\nSo all in all, it was a [[nerve-racking]] decision at the time, but I've [[never once regretted]] it.",
  why:["Смешанное условное: If I hadn't made that choice, I'd probably be sitting…","would для привычки в прошлом: I'd spend my evenings…","Идиомы: take the plunge, tip the balance, over the moon","Ясная структура: фон → проблема → как решал → почему важно → вывод"]},
 movie:{band:8, card:"movie", t:"Band 8 · Describe a movie you watched recently and enjoyed",
  text:"The film I'd like to talk about is The Intouchables, a French comedy-drama that I watched again a couple of weeks ago with my mum.\n\nI first saw it years ago at school, but I'd forgotten most of it, so when it [[popped up]] on a streaming service, we decided to [[give it another go]] on a rainy Sunday evening. It's based on a true story about a wealthy man who is paralysed after a paragliding accident and the young man from a poor suburb of Paris who [[ends up]] becoming his carer. At first they [[couldn't be more different]] — one loves classical music and art, the other is loud, cheeky and has never been to an opera — but gradually they become close friends.\n\n[[What I loved most about it]] was the balance between humour and emotion. [[One minute]] you're [[laughing your head off]], and [[the next]] you're [[on the verge of tears]]. The acting is [[outstanding]], especially from the carer, who brings so much energy to every scene. [[On top of that]], the soundtrack is absolutely beautiful.\n\nAs for why I enjoyed it so much, I think it's because it's [[a genuinely uplifting]] story about friendship and respect. It shows that people from completely different backgrounds can [[change each other's lives]], and that you shouldn't treat someone with a disability as if they were fragile. Watching it as an adult, I noticed far more than I did as a teenager.\n\nSo all in all, it's a film I'd [[recommend wholeheartedly]] — it's the perfect choice if you need cheering up.",
  why:["Сюжет пересказан коротко — главное оценка, а не пересказ","One minute…, the next… — живая связка","Present simple для сюжета, past для твоего опыта просмотра","Причастный оборот: Watching it as an adult, I noticed…"]},
 perfectjob:{band:8, card:"perfectjob", t:"Band 8 · Describe a perfect job you would like to have",
  text:"I'm going to tell you about what I'd consider my dream job, which is working as a translator for an international film festival.\n\n[[To give you some background]], I've been [[obsessed with]] cinema for as long as I can remember, and I've been learning English and Korean for several years now. A couple of years ago I volunteered at a small film festival in my city, and that's when I realised that this kind of work would be [[right up my street]].\n\nThe job would involve translating subtitles, interpreting at press conferences and helping foreign guests [[find their way around]]. It's definitely a [[demanding]] role — you need [[an excellent command of]] at least two languages, a lot of patience, and the ability to [[think on your feet]], because things go wrong at festivals all the time. You'd also need to be well organised, since you might be working with dozens of people at once.\n\nAs for why it would be perfect for me, it would combine the two things I love most: languages and films. I'm also quite sociable, so meeting directors and actors from all over the world would be [[a dream come true]]. [[Admittedly]], the hours would be long and the pay isn't always great, but for me [[job satisfaction]] matters far more than salary. If I could get even a short contract at a big festival, I'd [[jump at the chance]].\n\nSo all in all, it's a job that would [[play to my strengths]], and I'm doing everything I can to make it happen — which is exactly why I'm preparing for IELTS.",
  why:["would на протяжении всего рассказа — гипотетическое будущее","Present perfect continuous: I've been learning…","Уступка: Admittedly, … but…","Идиомы: right up my street, think on your feet, jump at the chance"]}
};

